[[SECTION]]
MID: 7dbd2d274a7e1b79e4b564b3bc5f04bc
UID: DOC-ARCHITECTURE-THREAT-MODEL-ownership-and-trust-boundaries
TITLE: Ownership and trust boundaries

[TEXT]
MID: eca588c456d91a5a6542b57c74a5ae55
STATEMENT: >>>
- **Clients:** Desktop Web is the current supported authoring product; native iOS is the accepted target with development and automatic Xcode validation paused and device acceptance incomplete. Their local draft journals are separate recoverable stores, not manuscript authorities. Mobile Web and the Capacitor authentication bridge are retired. The [LINK: DOC-ARCHITECTURE-IOS-SWIFTUI-ARCHITECTURE-7-implementation-slices-and-acceptance-gates, OAuth retirement procedure] covers historical registrations.
- **Cloudflare edge:** Workers Static Assets and Tunnel/API ingress route traffic. Access protects the UI entry and never substitutes for Fastify's principal, project, object and effect authorization.
- **Application and data:** Fastify admission, Better Auth, MCP, shared domain actions, the trusted optional module runtime and the PostgreSQL persistence adapter lead to PostgreSQL as the sole canonical data authority.
- **Host operations:** Protected GitHub Actions uses Tailscale OIDC, pinned SSH and Ansible to reach the approved host operations path. The web UI, API and MCP adapter do not reach SSH or Ansible.
- **Backup and offsite storage.** The existing host-locked `backup.sh` path handles PostgreSQL and history data. It transfers age-encrypted ciphertext to S3-compatible storage and verifies retrieved bytes and an independent protected receipt. Operational acceptance requires transfer and readback against the actual S3 destination and a restore on an independent recovery host. [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP, Backup]
- **Public Pages:** The existing Repository Audit Pages publisher exposes allowlisted static artifacts. The model treats this public boundary separately from the protected GitHub Actions workflow; G5 scans architecture inputs and generated public-source material for credential-like values.

These groupings describe trust relationships in the design. Actual Cloudflare, Tailscale, SSH, PostgreSQL, secret-store, S3 and restore-host configuration must be verified independently.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
MID: b7954e034a9578006571d7d14fc05f8a
UID: DOC-ARCHITECTURE-THREAT-MODEL-g5-static-verification
TITLE: G5 static verification

[TEXT]
MID: f1f9670b108e3ae5afe71e5e76407ad2
STATEMENT: >>>
The scoped verifier runs LikeC4 syntax/semantic validation and structural boundary checks, checks the native architecture view in the LikeC4 model export, analyzes the Threagile model with the pinned image, checks required risk instances and suppression state, and scans public architecture inputs for secret-like values.

It injects a wrong LikeC4 trust boundary and a Desktop Web-to-host-SSH relation into separate fixtures. LikeC4 parses and exports both fixtures before the structural checks must reject them. It also marks a required risk as mitigated and inserts a synthetic bearer value. Missing verification identifiers, both boundary violations, risk suppression and secret exposure must be rejected. This proves that the static gate detects those model faults; it does not prove the live API, OAuth, UI, host, backup or restore implementation.

Run from a clean source checkout:

```sh
npm ci --prefix scripts/architecture --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund
node scripts/architecture/verify.mjs "$CODEXIA_ARCHITECTURE_EVIDENCE_DIR"
```

`CODEXIA_ARCHITECTURE_EVIDENCE_DIR` must be outside the repository. The verifier writes the exact source SHA, input digests, tool identities and generated JSON outputs there. The evidence directory is not copied into Repository Audit Pages.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
